WEBVTT

00:00:00.548 --> 00:00:23.228
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. Saturday's Cariboo Local Affairs day, so we're staying close to home. Today we've got Budget 2026 and its $26.6 billion for Indigenous communities, the Stelco workers getting recalled on December 1st, and the Quesnel defamation case that got dismissed. Riley and I dig into whether a court ruling tells us who gets heard in a council chamber. Stay with us.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:54.871
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Saturday, November 14th, 2026, and today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:55.871 --> 00:01:27.973
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. We're looking at councils, budgets, and who gets heard in towns like Quesnel, Williams Lake, and 100 Mile House. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast and around 6, with a high of 11 and a low of 5 today. Light drizzle through the day, a 15 percent chance of precipitation, and about zero millimetres. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House and Williams Lake are both around 6 under overcast skies, Quesnel is around 5 with overcast, and Alexis Creek is around 6 and mainly clear.

00:01:28.173 --> 00:01:28.861
<v Casey>Now the news.

00:01:30.261 --> 00:02:19.907
<v Riley>Today's news is mostly about money, and the paperwork that comes after it. Canada's environment minister, Julie Dabrusin, defended the government's climate record as COP31 opened in Antalya, Türkiye. She pointed to methane action, electrification, and international climate finance. The sore spot is Ottawa's 2021 pledge to ban thermal coal exports. An environment commissioner's report said that work was abandoned in 2024 and formally ended in August 2025. Dabrusin said the previous government made that decision, and her government is open to next steps. Climate advocates say continued exports undercut Canada's place in the Powering Past Coal Alliance. Ottawa says it has committed $13 billion in climate support to developing countries over five years.

00:02:20.357 --> 00:02:24.349
<v Casey>Two governments, one pledge, and a commissioner's report doing the remembering.

00:02:26.101 --> 00:02:59.355
<v Casey>Budget 2026, released November 5th, allocates $26.6 billion over the next five fiscal years for Indigenous communities, and nearly $50 billion over ten. The Yellowhead Institute's review, written by Riley Yesno, calls it the largest total investment in Indigenous communities in any budget the review has looked at. It is more than five times the 2025 allocation of $9.29 billion. The review also says the money comes with expectations, and that the budget rests on a logic of debt. Several funding decisions carry what the review calls asterisks.

00:02:59.805 --> 00:03:05.341
<v Riley>Largest in the review's own count, and most of that is stretched over ten years instead of five.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:43.564
<v Riley>Cleveland-Cliffs says it will recall all 175 laid-off Stelco workers on December 1st, and repay their lost wages. The recall is at Stelco's finishing operations in Hamilton, about 70 kilometres southwest of Toronto. The company says bringing the cold-rolled and coated lines back will take several weeks. The United Steelworkers local's president says government pressure is what got the jobs restored. Industry Minister Mélanie Joly had warned that Ottawa could seek court-ordered compliance, penalties, or a forced sale over the takeover commitments. Cliffs denies breaching them.

00:03:45.033 --> 00:04:09.153
<v Casey>The federal budget extends the journalism tax credit by $501 million and expands it to audio and audiovisual news outlets. It also renews the Local Journalism Initiative, which funds reporting in underserved communities. A study earlier this year found reporters paid through that program believed they kept their watchdog role. Two-thirds of the interviewees were concerned about the perception of a conflict of interest.

00:04:09.603 --> 00:04:14.667
<v Riley>So the worry is in the study, and the money is in the budget. Those are two separate checks.

00:04:16.314 --> 00:04:58.512
<v Riley>A McGill University research centre tracked a network of nearly 300 TikTok and Instagram accounts. Together they posted more than 3,201 deepfake videos, which drew 22.1 million views over three months. Many of them were about Canada's trade dispute with the United States. The most-seen false story invented a 48-hour ultimatum from U.S. President Donald Trump, and claimed Prime Minister Mark Carney cancelled $8 billion in U.S. defence contracts in response. That story alone appeared in 161 videos and drew 5.1 million views. The researchers could not firmly attribute the network to anyone, and the accounts have not been monetized.

00:05:00.107 --> 00:05:29.457
<v Casey>Cuban authorities detained independent journalist Yoani Sánchez, founder and director of 14ymedio, on November 5th, after she left home to go shopping. She was released after several hours. The Committee to Protect Journalists calls her arrest part of a wider crackdown, with several other journalists held the same week. Among them was a contributor to CubaNet, a Miami-based exile news outlet. CPJ is calling on Cuban authorities to end the arrests and let journalists report without fear of retaliation.

00:05:30.858 --> 00:06:09.272
<v Riley>Serbia's student-backed list was projected to win a majority in parliament after former President Aleksandar Vučić conceded defeat in a snap election on October 25th. Vučić put the list at 135 to 140 of the 250 seats. One pollster put it at 138. Turnout reached 67.6 percent by 7 p.m., the highest since Yugoslavia broke up in 1992. The movement grew out of nearly two years of protests after a railway-station canopy collapsed in Novi Sad in November 2024. Its candidates have limited political experience, and the list has no single established leader.

00:06:09.722 --> 00:06:14.746
<v Casey>Projections from three weeks ago. Check the final count before anyone quotes these numbers.

00:06:16.554 --> 00:06:47.984
<v Casey>Government agencies in Broome County, New York, Idaho, and South Africa took websites offline, or swapped them for simplified versions, after warnings about critical flaws in PHP. The language runs nearly 70 percent of websites whose language can be identified. The U.S. FBI warned the flaws could let attackers run code remotely. The PHP Foundation released patches on Wednesday, and the FBI urged administrators to update or take systems offline until they can be secured. Broome County and South Africa's Presidency had not said when their sites would return.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:07:24.861
<v Riley>DreamHost confirmed that hackers stole customer emails and session tokens during an attack that began in August. A session token keeps someone logged in, so a stolen one lets an attacker act as that user. The web host says the intruders used an unspecified flaw in its file manager, and that the problem is unrelated to the PHP vulnerabilities. DreamHost says fewer than 10 percent of its customers were affected, but would not give a figure. It touts about 400,000 paying customers. The company did not say why it took until last week to detect the intrusion.

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:27.799
<v Casey>Under ten percent is still a lot of inboxes.

00:07:29.537 --> 00:07:38.985
<v Casey>Tom's Guide reports new research suggesting that living near an AI data centre might not hurt home values. The piece also covers electricity bills, water use, and noise.

00:07:40.575 --> 00:08:09.103
<v Riley>An analysis of trial data from 91,339 people, presented at ESC Congress 2026, found that missing blood pressure doses can cut much of the medication's protection against heart attacks and strokes. The lead presenter, Qianqian Yang of the University of Oxford, said earlier studies could be confounded by other factors, so the team used randomized trial data instead. About half of patients do not take their prescriptions as directed.

00:08:10.514 --> 00:08:40.136
<v Casey>A 24-year observational study of several thousand middle-aged and older Americans linked higher average BMI over time to faster declines in memory and thinking. The team, led by University of Georgia researcher Suhang Song, published in the Journal of Neurology. The link was strongest when cognitive tests came about eight years after the weight measurements. Participants reported their own height and weight, and the analysis could not account for physical activity. The study cannot show that weight causes the decline.

00:08:40.586 --> 00:08:45.538
<v Riley>Self-reported weight over twenty-four years. That caveat carries the whole study.

00:08:47.357 --> 00:09:17.637
<v Riley>South Dakota State University researchers found that shearing pregnant ewes around day 50 of gestation may produce heavier lambs at birth, especially in ewes carrying twins or triplets. The researchers link the effect to a mild cold stress that may affect placental development and blood flow. Researcher Alex Vasquez-Hidalgo says some cold stress appears beneficial to the pregnant ewe. Cold-climate producers are being urged to weigh that carefully before changing their shearing schedule.

00:09:19.072 --> 00:09:45.080
<v Casey>Dublin, Ontario farmer Greg Beuermann has been no-tilling soybeans for 35 years, and he calls corn residue the biggest challenge to a good soybean stand. He is now testing whether chopping corn stalks improves planting conditions. Ontario Ministry soybean specialist Horst Bohner visited his farm for RealAgriculture's Soybean School. Earlier on-farm trials showed a 2.8 to 3.3 bushel-per-acre yield advantage when the coulters ran deep.

00:09:46.486 --> 00:10:29.236
<v Riley>The Netherlands awarded €783.5 million in subsidies to seven green hydrogen projects in 2022. Together they were supposed to deliver 1,150 megawatts of electrolysis capacity. Four years later, four projects totalling 550 megawatts have been cancelled, and none of the seven is operating. Parliamentary answers published November 2nd say the government cannot say when electrolytic hydrogen becomes commercially viable without dedicated subsidies. Another €780 million is reserved for a future round. The €783.5 million was awarded, not spent, and roughly €150 million had gone out when those answers came out.

00:10:29.685 --> 00:10:37.277
<v Casey>Money committed before anyone can say when the plan pays off. First, a local organization that works at the other end of hard decisions.

00:10:38.677 --> 00:11:00.277
<v Riley>CMHA Cariboo Chilcotin provides counselling, housing support, victim services, and crisis intervention in Williams Lake and 100 Mile House. If you're working through something hard, or you know someone who is, their work runs from a first conversation to helping someone keep a roof over their head. You can find them at cariboo.cmha.bc.ca.

00:11:00.802 --> 00:11:06.034
<v Casey>Services close to home, run by people who answer the phone. That's the standard we should expect.

00:11:06.365 --> 00:11:09.581
<v Riley>A Quesnel court outcome next, and the question underneath it.

00:11:10.981 --> 00:11:30.557
<v Riley>Pat Morton's defamation case against Quesnel City Councillor Laurey-Anne Roodenburg has been dismissed, according to My Cariboo Now. When people who have given years to a town get the first call on the room where decisions happen, is that fair? Does a court case show whether a newcomer with no history gets the same room?

00:11:30.869 --> 00:11:37.549
<v Casey>The item gives us a headline, a one-line summary, and three office addresses. The phone numbers are the longest thing in it.

00:11:38.062 --> 00:12:12.140
<v Riley>Start with the mechanism, then. Defamation lets someone sue over a statement that damages their reputation. The defences for public comment include fair comment and qualified privilege. An application to dismiss lets a judge end a case before trial, and it is cheaper than a full trial by design. That application was heard on May 12th, in Quesnel, before B.C. Supreme Court Justice Ronald Tindale. The Free Press reported on May 26th that Roodenburg's lawyer, Anja Nel, argued the suit aims to "control the narrative" and suppress political expression.

00:12:12.661 --> 00:12:19.061
<v Casey>Those are the defence's arguments. A dismissal tells us the judge agreed with something. It does not tell us which thing.

00:12:19.718 --> 00:12:47.246
<v Riley>The written reasons are where you'd check, and I wouldn't say more about why until someone has read them. Nel also argued that the public interest in Roodenburg's expression "far outweighs" the public interest in letting the suit proceed. Her case rested on fair comment and qualified privilege. She argued Morton couldn't show harm, or that any harm came from Roodenburg's statements, and that Morton had invited criticism through her own conduct.

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:58.780
<v Casey>Invited criticism is a claim about the plaintiff's behaviour, which is a different question from whether the councillor's words were fair. A judge can decide the first without touching the second.

00:12:59.342 --> 00:13:04.366
<v Riley>So the outcome is the firm part. A claim over statements on a public matter was dismissed.

00:13:04.998 --> 00:13:27.670
<v Casey>Public matter. The statements were about a book. *Grave Error: How the Media Misled Us (and the Truth About Residential Schools)*. It questions the 2015 Truth and Reconciliation Commission report and the unverified 2021 Kamloops claims. Roodenburg, the council's Indigenous relations liaison, criticized Morton at a council meeting. A recording of that meeting was played in court.

00:13:28.284 --> 00:13:45.220
<v Riley>So the public part is a council meeting, about a contested book, with the liaison who handles reconciliation doing the criticizing. That sits close to the centre of what this protection is for. If councillors couldn't talk about contested things in public, the chamber would only hear one side.

00:13:45.737 --> 00:14:01.521
<v Casey>Morton's notice of civil claim calls the statements "defamatory, but malicious, politically motivated." Defamation is the first accusation. Malice and motive are the second, and motive is very hard to prove in either direction. A judge doesn't have to read anyone's heart to rule on the words.

00:14:02.101 --> 00:14:14.317
<v Riley>And the protection doesn't depend on who's speaking. The test is whether the statement is about a public matter. A newcomer who says the same thing at the same meeting is in the same legal position as the councillor.

00:14:14.917 --> 00:14:28.557
<v Casey>Same test, different cost of reaching it. The councillor had a lawyer, a hearing, and a seat that gets her heard at the next meeting. A resident with no seat has to pay a lawyer before anyone reads the statement, and the plaintiff picks the moment.

00:14:29.180 --> 00:14:35.812
<v Riley>The application is the cheap route, by design. It exists so a defendant doesn't have to pay for a full trial to win.

00:14:36.399 --> 00:14:42.447
<v Casey>Cheaper than a trial, still a hearing with a lawyer on each side. The May hearing was a cost, and the defendant paid it.

00:14:43.059 --> 00:14:52.715
<v Riley>The defendant, who then won. A resident can be sued the same way, and the same application is open to them. The rule doesn't check the family tree at the door.

00:14:53.343 --> 00:15:03.463
<v Casey>Open on paper. Whether it's open in practice depends on who can pay the first bill. Nothing in the reporting tells us how many people drop a statement rather than face that bill.

00:15:04.072 --> 00:15:10.280
<v Riley>That's an argument about deterrence, and I'd want the number before accepting it. I don't have it. Do you?

00:15:10.556 --> 00:15:15.244
<v Casey>No. I'd want to know how many claims in the Morton record ever reached a judgment at all.

00:15:15.754 --> 00:15:39.194
<v Riley>The Tyee reports Morton has filed more than a dozen civil claims in B.C. courts over several decades. Her suit against the Union of B.C. Indian Chiefs was also thrown out, by Justice Jasmin Ahmad. Ahmad found that the public interest in preserving free debate outweighed the harm Morton claimed, and ordered her to pay the union's costs. Two dismissals, two judges, and the same logic each time.

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:51.266
<v Casey>Two judges, two defendants, and one plaintiff who files often. The dismissals tell you about the claims. The filing count tells you something about the litigant. Neither one decides the case in front of Tindale.

00:15:51.936 --> 00:15:59.040
<v Riley>More than a dozen claims over decades is a count, not a verdict. Plenty of people file and lose. Filing isn't a crime.

00:15:59.607 --> 00:16:07.079
<v Casey>No. Still, I want the list of outcomes, not just the number of filings. A pattern needs to show which claims survived the first hearing.

00:16:07.613 --> 00:16:17.453
<v Riley>The court registry can produce that list. I'd resist treating a count as evidence the councillor was in the wrong. The dismissals say the judges didn't think so.

00:16:17.995 --> 00:16:24.715
<v Casey>The dismissals say the judges didn't find the claims worth a trial. That's narrower than vindicating the councillor's choice of words.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:37.146
<v Riley>Narrower, yes. But it's the same narrow test every resident faces. If the words are about a public matter and the claim can't show harm, the case ends early. A Quesnel resident can read that rule.

00:16:37.798 --> 00:17:00.646
<v Casey>If they can read it. Both sides are also pointing at the same history. Paull told reporters he did not agree with what his wife was doing, and he cited his own reconciliation work, including co-hosting the 2024 B.C. Winter Games with the Lhtako Dene Nation. So the mayor's household points at reconciliation on one side, and the councillor who handles reconciliation points at it on the other.

00:17:01.323 --> 00:17:10.515
<v Riley>The book criticizes the TRC report. The councillor objected to the book. Each side can claim the same history as its own argument, and a court can't settle that.

00:17:11.054 --> 00:17:19.054
<v Casey>It can't. A court can decide whether a claim goes to trial. It can't tell a council how to talk about residential schools. The headline leaves that out.

00:17:19.954 --> 00:17:34.770
<v Riley>Which makes it a governance question. A council will disagree in the chamber. The useful question is whether it has agreed rules for how members describe a resident by name, and whether a councillor gets the same rules as a member of the public.

00:17:35.441 --> 00:17:49.553
<v Casey>I don't know whether Quesnel has those rules written down. I'd check the procedure bylaw before assuming it does. We covered Quesnel's new council on Tuesday, and a new council is when habits either get written into the rules or get left to the chair.

00:17:50.076 --> 00:18:03.636
<v Riley>Quesnel is a community of about 9,000. A councillor and the former mayor's household sit on opposite sides of a dispute about a book. Everyone in town is going to run into both of them at the grocery store.

00:18:04.202 --> 00:18:13.010
<v Casey>And at the next council meeting. The people who heard that recording are still in the room. So are the people who didn't hear it and will get a version from someone at the coffee shop.

00:18:13.541 --> 00:18:24.405
<v Riley>That's the real cost, and it isn't a legal one. A resident who wants to criticize a councillor will look at how the last case went, and see a long local name and a lawyer on each side.

00:18:24.969 --> 00:18:34.473
<v Casey>The fairness problem is who gets to set the terms. Long service earns a seat at the table. It doesn't earn the right to call a resident's motives malicious in the record.

00:18:35.106 --> 00:18:46.418
<v Riley>Nobody in the material says long service earns that right. The defendant won with the same test any resident gets. The plaintiff, who has the longest local name in the file, lost twice.

00:18:47.021 --> 00:18:58.421
<v Casey>Lost twice on a motion to dismiss. That's a pleading decision. And the family is the one who brought the case into court in the first place. A newcomer with the same grievance may not have had that option.

00:18:58.968 --> 00:19:07.272
<v Riley>Then measure it. Ask how many people in Quesnel have been sued over something said at a council meeting. Ask the court registry, not the newspaper.

00:19:07.825 --> 00:19:17.465
<v Casey>Fine. And ask whether the reasons get read into the public record. If the only account is a one-line item and three phone numbers, a resident has a headline and no ruling.

00:19:18.126 --> 00:19:30.590
<v Riley>Written reasons are normally public. If the decision is on the court's record, a resident can read it in an afternoon, and so can any councillor. The barrier is whether anyone bothers to read it and explain it.

00:19:31.199 --> 00:19:40.215
<v Casey>The court publishes, the council hears about it in the agenda package, and the paper covers the result. Each one can assume another is explaining it. None of them has to.

00:19:40.742 --> 00:19:53.134
<v Riley>So who should explain it? The council could table it at a public meeting. The paper could put the reasons next to the headline. The court can post the decision. Pick one, and the other two stop being excuses.

00:19:53.715 --> 00:20:01.931
<v Casey>I'd pick the council. It governs the rule, and it's the body still sitting there after the court date. A resident can ask a question out loud there.

00:20:02.576 --> 00:20:15.208
<v Riley>The council doesn't have to rule on the case. It can put a plain question to its procedure bylaw: does a sitting member get the same protection and the same limits as a resident when they speak about a named person?

00:20:15.535 --> 00:20:23.479
<v Casey>And if the answer is "we're not sure," the council should say so out loud. That beats a press release that says nothing about how the decision was made.

00:20:24.038 --> 00:20:36.278
<v Riley>I think the rule works as written. The dismissals are real, and Morton's claims didn't survive two judges. You think nobody has checked whether it works for someone without a family name. We won't settle that today.

00:20:36.803 --> 00:20:48.059
<v Casey>No. I'd leave the question open, because nobody has measured it. A rule can be fair on paper and still be expensive in practice, and the people who pay are the ones who can't afford to defend a statement.

00:20:48.656 --> 00:21:04.192
<v Riley>Then put one question to the registry and the other to the council: would the rule that protected a councillor protect a resident who said the same thing at the same meeting? If you have a case from your own town, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:21:04.858 --> 00:21:11.466
<v Casey>Ask it in public, where the answer can be checked. Read the ruling before you repeat the headline, and enjoy the weekend.

00:21:11.744 --> 00:21:14.272
<v Riley>Good weekend. Bring a pen to the next council meeting.

00:21:14.871 --> 00:21:33.895
<v Riley>Cariboo Signals is produced by Erich Zirnhelt — scripts by Claude, today's voices by OpenAI TTS, theme by Suno. Weather data from Open-Meteo. Today's episode included additional web research via Brave Search and Kagi. Automated with GitHub Actions, hosted on Cloudflare Pages. Find us at cariboo signals dot c-a.